Description: hw/arm: Add the Chumby Classic machine
 The Chumby Classic: an i.MX21 with 64 MiB of SDRAM, 64 MiB of NAND flash
 (-drive if=mtd), a 320x240 LCD with a TSC2100 touchscreen and audio
 codec on CSPI3 and its pen interrupt on GPIO PE19, the crypto processor
 on UART3, the sensor card on CSPI1 and the board's lines.
 .
 It boots a zImage directly as an MX21ADS (machine type 851), the type
 the Chumby's own kernel is built for, with the stock command line unless
 -append is given. The device's own 2.6.16 kernel and root disk run
 unmodified to its control panel.
 .
 - Audio: the codec's link goes to the AUDMUX's peripheral ports (its
   DIN on port 2 and DOUT on port 1, as the stock driver routes them),
   with the usual "audiodev" machine property.
 - Board lines: a chumby-io device takes the PWM's output, the keypad
   port's rows and columns and the LCD's brightness. Its properties
   (button, button-key, headphones, usb-keyboard) are the machine's too,
   so -M chumby-classic,button-key=pause and qom-set /machine headphones
   true work.
 - Identity: the guid (XXXXXXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXXXXXXXXXX, 32 hex
   digits), serial (16 letters or digits) and hwversion (MAJOR.MINOR,
   each 0 to 255) properties go to the crypto processor; the hardware
   version also goes on the default kernel command line, as
   chumby_hwversion=. The stock software takes the device ID from the
   GUID ("cpi -p", via guidgen.sh, in download_cp's control panel
   request), so each emulated Chumby should have its own. A malformed
   value is refused at startup with an error saying what was expected.
   The identity is fixed once the machine is created: the parts were
   given it then, so a later set is refused rather than reported
   without the guest seeing it.
 - Sensor card: the stock kernel's chumby_accel module drives it on
   CSPI1 (SPI_CHAN 0). The card's two select lines are GPIOs, PD28 (SS0)
   and PD27 (SS1), which the driver sets by hand and the card decodes
   into four chip selects: SS1:SS0 = 2 is the Kionix accelerometer, 1
   the Atmel AT25080A "DC ID" EEPROM, and 3 is idle, which the driver
   always passes through between the two. A decoder on the board drives
   the parts' chip selects from the GPIO outputs, and CSPI1 hands them
   bytes (its "bytewise" mode). A machine has no vmsd, so the board
   registers a small VMState of its own, "chumby-classic", for the
   decoder (a value of 4 or more is refused; a state without it loads
   with the decoder idle); the parts' own state says whether each is
   selected, so the decoder's outputs are not driven again on load.
 - Accelerometer: /machine/accel. The machine properties accel-x,
   accel-y and accel-z, in milli-g, set its x-mg, y-mg and z-mg, at
   startup (-M) or at run time (qom-set /machine accel-x 500). They start
   at 0, 0, 1000: 1 g on Z, as for the part lying flat. Which way up the
   card sits in a Chumby isn't known; that is a guess, and the stock
   software doesn't settle it.
 - DC ID EEPROM: contents in the stock layouts, consistent with the
   identity. 0x300 holds the revision, 00.00.MAJOR.MINOR from the
   hardware version, and 0x304 the last 12 characters of the serial
   number, which the driver reads into /proc/DCversion. 0x000 holds what
   the stock "dcid" tool reads (dcid -o, sent with the control panel
   request by download_cp): the magic "sexi", one tag and "pus!", a tag
   being a big-endian 16-bit length with bit 7 marking a container, a
   4-character name and its tags or value bytes. The tags are made up,
   as no real card's contents are known:
   <dcid><vers>01</vers><hwvr>MAJOR MINOR</hwvr></dcid>
